Dynamic bottleneck prediction is important to digital manufacturing because timely knowledge of a changing throughput constraint enables production intervention before its effects spread across the line. The task is to determine which resource will constrain throughput and to characterize when the event begins, how long it persists, and what observable production condition explains it. Existing studies are limited by the scarcity of bottleneck events in physical factories and by data that predominantly describe machines while underrepresenting workers, buffers, materials, and logistics. Consequently, conventional time-series models and local graph predictors may not fully capture heterogeneous cross-resource interaction or the delayed propagation of a disturbance into a remote station-level constraint. This paper proposes \textbf{BNPDFormer}, a delay-aware spatiotemporal Transformer for fine-grained dynamic bottleneck prediction. A disturbance-controlled digital production line generates traceable human-, material-, machine-, and logistics-related trajectories without disrupting physical production. Process-informed labelling and clustering distinguish sustained bottleneck regimes from normal production patterns, improving data separability and providing operational evidence for cause prediction. A heterogeneous factory graph preserves typed process and logistics relations, while recurrent short-term patterns condition spatiotemporal interaction so that lagged precursors can contribute to prediction. BNPDFormer jointly reports the bottleneck station, onset, duration, process cause, and remaining order time at minute resolution. Experiments show that clustering improves event and cause prediction, graph modelling strengthens relational learning and interpretability, and the integrated BNPDFormer outperforms the compared baselines across station identification, early warning, timing, and diagnosis. These results support a more accurate, fine-grained, and interpretable approach to dynamic bottleneck prediction in complex digital production systems.

\zhpara{动态瓶颈预测对于数字制造具有重要意义，因为及时获知不断变化的吞吐约束，能够使生产干预在其影响扩散至整条产线之前实施。该任务不仅需要判断未来由哪一资源限制吞吐量，还需要说明事件何时开始、持续多久，以及何种可观测生产状态能够解释该事件。现有研究受到两方面限制：实体工厂中的瓶颈事件样本稀缺；可获取数据又主要描述机器，对人员、缓冲区、物料和物流的表征不足。因此，传统时间序列模型和局部图预测方法难以充分捕捉异构资源之间的交互，以及局部扰动经过一定时延后演变为远端工位约束的过程。为此，本文提出 \textbf{BNPDFormer}，一种面向细粒度动态瓶颈预测的延迟感知时空 Transformer。可控扰动的数字化生产线在不干扰实体生产的条件下生成可追溯的人员、物料、机器和物流轨迹。过程启发式标签与聚类相结合，用于区分持续性瓶颈工况与正常生产模式，从而提高数据可分性，并为原因预测提供运营证据。异构工厂图保留具有类型差异的工艺和物流关系，重复出现的短时模式进一步调节时空交互，使具有时滞的前驱信息能够参与预测。BNPDFormer 以分钟为分辨率，联合报告瓶颈工位、开始时间、持续时间、过程原因和订单剩余时间。实验表明，聚类提高了事件与原因预测准确率，图建模增强了关系学习能力与可解释性，而完整 BNPDFormer 在工位识别、提前预警、时间预测和原因诊断等指标上优于对照基线。上述结果为复杂数字生产系统提供了一种更准确、更细粒度且更具可解释性的动态瓶颈预测方法。}
